export function encodeRow(fields) {
  return fields.map((field) => {
    const escaped = field.replace(/"/g, '""');
    return /[",\r\n]/.test(field) ? `"${escaped}"` : escaped;
  }).join(',');
}

# User cancellation plus a 5-second timeout

This browser example lets either a user action or a timeout stop the same operation:

```html
<button id="cancel">Cancel</button>
<script>
const controller = new AbortController();
document.querySelector("#cancel").onclick = () =>
  controller.abort(new DOMException("User cancelled", "AbortError"));

const timeout = AbortSignal.timeout(5000);
const signal = AbortSignal.any([controller.signal, timeout]);

(async () => {
  try {
    const response = await fetch("/slow-resource", { signal });
    console.log(await response.text());
  } catch (error) {
    // Read the combined signal: it identifies the winning source.
    const reason = signal.reason;
    if (reason?.name === "TimeoutError") {
      console.log("Timed out after 5 seconds");
    } else if (reason?.name === "AbortError") {
      console.log("Cancelled by the user");
    } else {
      console.error("Other failure", error);
    }
  }
})();
</script>
```

`AbortSignal.timeout(5000)` creates a signal that automatically aborts after 5,000 **active** milliseconds and uses a `TimeoutError` `DOMException` as its reason. `AbortSignal.any([...])` returns a signal that aborts when any input aborts; its reason is the reason of the first input signal to abort. Thus, if the button wins, `signal.reason.name` is `AbortError`; if the timeout wins, it is `TimeoutError`. If an input is already aborted, that input can determine the combined signal immediately.

**Inference:** checking `signal.reason` is the reliable way in this example to classify which input won; the sources establish the reason-selection rule, while applying it to the two names above follows from the code and the documented timeout reason. The caught `error` should still be retained for diagnostics because the operation may report its own failure.

“5 seconds” is not necessarily five seconds of wall-clock time: MDN says the timeout uses active time and can pause while a worker is suspended or a document is in the back-forward cache (bfcache).

